1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:04,205
Bankable is not enough. Episode one: The Signing.

2
00:00:06,600 --> 00:00:07,837
Meet the cast.

3
00:00:09,950 --> 00:00:17,886
Belpau. Analyst at KivoElec, the national utility. Curious and stubborn. Reads every clause, even at midnight.

4
00:00:19,486 --> 00:00:28,723
Enilec Mok. Director of Public Debt at the Ministry of Finance. Guards the public purse. For her, a guarantee is a debt with a delay.

5
00:00:30,323 --> 00:00:36,915
Minister Kerbu. Minister of Energy. Wants power for his people, and fast. Under pressure every day.

6
00:00:38,515 --> 00:00:46,515
Emson Anahct. CEO of SunRiver Power. Serious and ambitious. Needs contracts that banks will finance.

7
00:00:48,115 --> 00:00:58,163
Tidianie Eugom. Investment officer at a development finance institution. Lends to private projects. Honest about who really carries the risk.

8
00:00:59,763 --> 00:01:07,550
Paul Ahmak. Managing Director of KivoElec. Runs a utility short of cash. Knows the grid better than anyone.

9
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:10,963
Chapter one. The promise.

10
00:01:12,563 --> 00:01:15,144
Kivona. Another night without power.

11
00:01:15,444 --> 00:01:17,065
Resident: Again?! The third time this week!

12
00:01:18,665 --> 00:01:24,980
Belpau studies by the light of her phone. One day, she thinks, this country will have power every night.

13
00:01:26,580 --> 00:01:32,233
Clinics and shops run on diesel generators: expensive, noisy and polluting.

14
00:01:33,833 --> 00:01:35,476
Then, the good news.

15
00:01:35,776 --> 00:01:41,024
The Kivona Herald. Minister promises one hundred megawatts of solar power.

16
00:01:42,624 --> 00:01:48,000
Minister Kerbu: Today Kivona signs with SunRiver Power. One hundred megawatts of light for our people!

17
00:01:49,600 --> 00:01:51,499
Emson Anahct: We are ready to build, Minister.

18
00:01:53,099 --> 00:01:58,517
An MoU is a promise. Financial close is where the promise gets paid for.

19
00:02:00,117 --> 00:02:01,461
Decision note.

20
00:02:01,761 --> 00:02:10,636
Across Africa, many power MoUs never reach financial close. Those that do are only as strong as the contracts behind them.

21
00:02:12,236 --> 00:02:14,263
Emson Anahct: Signed! Now we can raise the money.

22
00:02:14,563 --> 00:02:18,381
Tidianie Eugom: An MoU is a good start, Emson. It is not financing.

23
00:02:19,981 --> 00:02:33,997
Tidianie Eugom: What our credit committee will check. Can the buyer pay? The utility's finances. Is the PPA solid and fair? Can the grid take the power? Land, permits and environmental impact. And who carries the currency risk?

24
00:02:35,597 --> 00:02:38,157
Emson Anahct: Then Kivona must give us strong guarantees.

25
00:02:38,457 --> 00:02:42,660
Tidianie Eugom: Or build strong institutions. Guarantees are never free, Emson.

26
00:02:44,260 --> 00:02:45,732
Red team question.

27
00:02:46,032 --> 00:02:50,491
What commitment did we just create, and what does it cost to walk away from it?

28
00:02:51,941 --> 00:02:54,287
Chapter two. What lenders need.

29
00:02:55,887 --> 00:03:06,298
Emson Anahct: Minister, lenders will need a bankable revenue contract and adequate protection against risks that could interrupt debt service. For this project, three things.

30
00:03:07,898 --> 00:03:21,999
Emson Anahct: One: a long-term PPA. Twenty years or more of guaranteed purchase. Two: a dollar tariff, so the lenders carry no currency risk. Three: a State guarantee, in case the utility cannot pay.

31
00:03:22,299 --> 00:03:29,169
IPPs bring private capital, speed and skills. The question is not whether to do the deal, but how.

32
00:03:30,769 --> 00:03:33,393
Minister Kerbu: Give him what he needs. Our people want light now!

33
00:03:34,993 --> 00:03:36,337
Decision note.

34
00:03:36,637 --> 00:03:53,810
IPPs are part of the solution; the goal is balanced deals, not fewer deals. Lenders need an adequate revenue and credit support structure; the instruments depend on the project's risk profile. What Emson lists are common forms, not universal requirements.

35
00:03:55,410 --> 00:03:59,698
Emson Anahct: I am not asking for favours. Without these, no bank will lend.

36
00:04:01,298 --> 00:04:03,773
Minister Kerbu: Paul? The PPA must be approved by Friday.

37
00:04:05,373 --> 00:04:08,722
Paul Ahmak: Friday? Minister, we have not even seen the grid study.

38
00:04:10,322 --> 00:04:17,319
Political calendars move faster than contracts. Rushed negotiations are where hidden costs are born.

39
00:04:18,919 --> 00:04:20,391
Red team question.

40
00:04:20,691 --> 00:04:24,403
What must be true before Friday, and who has actually checked it?

41
00:04:25,853 --> 00:04:28,029
Chapter three. The fine print.

42
00:04:29,629 --> 00:04:32,723
Paul Ahmak: Belpau, the Ministry wants our comments by Friday.

43
00:04:33,023 --> 00:04:35,135
Belpau: Friday?! It is three hundred pages!

44
00:04:36,735 --> 00:04:40,639
Belpau: We pay for what the plant can produce... even when our grid cannot take it?

45
00:04:42,239 --> 00:04:51,028
Clause seven point two. Capacity payment. The Offtaker shall pay for the Available Capacity, whether or not the energy is dispatched.

46
00:04:52,628 --> 00:05:06,239
Belpau: Questions for Monday. One: take or pay. We pay even if we cannot use the power. Two: a tariff in U.S. dollars. Three: a State guarantee with no ceiling. Four: who pays if the grid is late?

47
00:05:07,839 --> 00:05:09,311
Red team question.

48
00:05:09,611 --> 00:05:15,093
What exactly are we paying for: capacity, energy, availability or dispatch?

49
00:05:16,693 --> 00:05:27,829
Take or pay: the utility pays for available capacity. A one hundred megawatt solar plant. A grid that can absorb sixty. Forty megawatts: paid, never used.

50
00:05:29,429 --> 00:05:34,805
Belpau: Our grid can absorb sixty megawatts today. We would pay for forty megawatts we cannot use.

51
00:05:36,405 --> 00:05:41,547
Paul Ahmak: And the line upgrade we need for the full one hundred megawatts is not financed yet.

52
00:05:43,147 --> 00:05:44,491
Decision note.

53
00:05:44,791 --> 00:06:03,564
Take or pay is common and protects lenders; it is not bad in itself. Its risk depends on what is paid for, what triggers payment, the caps and exceptions, and who controls the underlying risk. Signing it before the grid, the demand and the utility's cash flow are ready is where it bites.

54
00:06:05,164 --> 00:06:13,271
KivoElec. For every one hundred billed, seventy are collected, and ninety-five are needed to pay costs and IPPs.

55
00:06:14,871 --> 00:06:17,175
Paul Ahmak: We already pay some of our suppliers late.

56
00:06:17,475 --> 00:06:19,459
Belpau: So if we miss a payment to SunRiver...

57
00:06:19,759 --> 00:06:23,705
Paul Ahmak: ...the State guarantee is called, and the bill goes to the Treasury.

58
00:06:25,305 --> 00:06:26,649
Decision note.

59
00:06:26,949 --> 00:06:35,141
The strongest guarantee is a utility that can pay. Fixing collection and cash flow reduces the need for State support.

60
00:06:36,591 --> 00:06:38,725
Chapter four. The hidden bill.

61
00:06:40,325 --> 00:06:43,013
Enilec Mok: A State guarantee? That is not in our budget.

62
00:06:43,313 --> 00:06:45,873
Belpau: Not yet. It is a contingent liability.

63
00:06:47,473 --> 00:06:58,118
Enilec Mok: A guarantee needs almost no cash on the day we sign. But the contingent exposure starts when the guarantee is issued, and it can carry fees. That is exactly why it is dangerous.

64
00:06:58,418 --> 00:07:07,421
On the books: public debt, salaries, schools and roads. Off the books, for now: State guarantees, take or pay commitments, and currency risk.

65
00:07:09,021 --> 00:07:10,493
Red team question.

66
00:07:10,793 --> 00:07:16,659
If the guarantee is called next year, where does the money come from, and what else goes unpaid?

67
00:07:18,259 --> 00:07:21,779
What the signing ceremony shows: a bankable project.

68
00:07:22,079 --> 00:07:28,863
What the Treasury may pay later: currency risk. Take or pay. The State guarantee. Utility arrears.

69
00:07:30,463 --> 00:07:31,807
Decision note.

70
00:07:32,107 --> 00:07:47,659
A contingent liability is a potential obligation whose timing or amount depends on an uncertain future event. It may not be recorded as debt today, but it must be identified, measured and disclosed before signing, not after.

71
00:07:49,259 --> 00:07:54,486
Enilec Mok: Tariff in dollars, revenue in local currency. If our currency falls, who pays the difference?

72
00:07:54,786 --> 00:07:55,661
Belpau: The Treasury.

73
00:07:57,261 --> 00:08:14,690
Year one: one dollar buys six hundred Kivona francs. A tariff of ten U.S. cents per kilowatt-hour costs sixty francs. Year five: one dollar buys eight hundred francs, and the same tariff costs eighty. Same tariff in dollars. One third more in local money.

74
00:08:16,290 --> 00:08:19,234
Enilec Mok: Belpau, write it all down. I want to meet their lender.

75
00:08:19,534 --> 00:08:20,451
Belpau: Already done.

76
00:08:22,051 --> 00:08:23,523
Red team question.

77
00:08:23,823 --> 00:08:30,373
What does this contract cost the utility after a forty percent depreciation? Who has run that number?

78
00:08:31,823 --> 00:08:34,191
Chapter five. Bankable, for whom?

79
00:08:35,791 --> 00:08:39,588
Tidianie Eugom: From our side, the project is bankable. Our loan is protected.

80
00:08:41,188 --> 00:08:42,575
Enilec Mok: Protected by whom?

81
00:08:44,188 --> 00:08:45,425
Tidianie Eugom: Well... by you.

82
00:08:47,188 --> 00:08:53,545
Tidianie Eugom: Lenders price risk. When the State carries it, the deal looks cheap. The cost is simply hidden elsewhere.

83
00:08:53,845 --> 00:08:55,979
Enilec Mok: Then let us put that cost on the table.

84
00:08:57,579 --> 00:09:02,592
A deal can be bankable for the project, and still unsustainable for the country.

85
00:09:04,192 --> 00:09:07,200
Enilec Mok: Around this table, who defends the country's balance sheet?

86
00:09:08,800 --> 00:09:11,040
Belpau: To be frank, nobody trained us for this.

87
00:09:12,640 --> 00:09:13,984
Decision note.

88
00:09:14,284 --> 00:09:23,329
Bankability asks: will the lenders be repaid? Sustainability asks: can the country keep paying? Negotiators need to answer both.

89
00:09:24,779 --> 00:09:27,382
Chapter six. Around the same table.

90
00:09:28,982 --> 00:09:30,817
Minister Kerbu: Are you trying to kill my project?!

91
00:09:31,117 --> 00:09:33,271
Enilec Mok: No, Minister. We are trying to save it.

92
00:09:34,871 --> 00:09:44,919
Belpau: Three risks in this PPA. One: take or pay before the grid is ready. Two: full exposure to the dollar. Three: a State guarantee with no ceiling.

93
00:09:46,519 --> 00:09:51,469
Minister Kerbu: Call everyone: developer, lender, utility, Finance. Same table, next week.

94
00:09:53,069 --> 00:09:54,541
Red team question.

95
00:09:54,841 --> 00:10:02,307
What happens to this deal if demand does not materialise, or if the cheaper hydro plant is dispatched first?

96
00:10:03,907 --> 00:10:06,681
Instead of fighting the deal, they fixed it.

97
00:10:06,981 --> 00:10:08,559
Minister Kerbu: Let us fix this deal together.

98
00:10:10,159 --> 00:10:14,831
Paul Ahmak: First, we fix our cash flow: meters, collection, and a payment reserve.

99
00:10:16,431 --> 00:10:20,890
Belpau: We phase the plant with the grid upgrade. No paying for power we cannot use.

100
00:10:22,490 --> 00:10:28,378
Tidianie Eugom: We can add a partial risk guarantee and a local currency loan. Less risk stays with the State.

101
00:10:29,978 --> 00:10:34,607
Enilec Mok: And every guarantee goes into a register, with a ceiling and a yearly risk report.

102
00:10:36,207 --> 00:10:39,834
Emson Anahct: A deal that breaks my client breaks me too. Count me in.

103
00:10:40,134 --> 00:10:41,094
Minister Kerbu: Then we have a deal.

104
00:10:42,694 --> 00:11:02,321
The new deal. Capacity: sixty megawatts now, forty when the grid is ready. Currency: a local currency loan for part of the debt. Guarantee: a capped guarantee, plus a partial risk guarantee from the lender. Utility: a payment reserve and a collection plan. Finance: every commitment in a risk register.

105
00:11:02,621 --> 00:11:06,930
Same plant. Same developer. Same lenders. A different deal.

106
00:11:08,530 --> 00:11:10,002
Red team question.

107
00:11:10,302 --> 00:11:16,254
If this contract terminates in year three, what does the State owe, and where is that number written?

108
00:11:17,854 --> 00:11:21,225
Paul Ahmak: A joint team: Finance, utility, regulator.

109
00:11:21,525 --> 00:11:24,469
Enilec Mok: Next time, we will be ready before the MoU is signed.

110
00:11:24,769 --> 00:11:26,219
Belpau: And training for all of us.

111
00:11:27,819 --> 00:11:38,913
Before the next MoU: one team for the public side. The regulator, the Ministry of Energy, the utility and the Ministry of Finance, working as one joint deal team.

112
00:11:40,513 --> 00:11:41,857
Decision note.

113
00:11:42,157 --> 00:12:01,421
The tools exist: phasing, liquidity reserves, partial risk guarantees, local currency finance and fiscal risk registers. Using them well requires trained people on the public side, and for large deals, an independent technical, financial, legal, and environmental and social review.

114
00:12:02,871 --> 00:12:05,367
Chapter seven. Financial close.

115
00:12:06,967 --> 00:12:10,508
Months later. Financial close: Kivona Solar.

116
00:12:10,808 --> 00:12:12,707
Minister Kerbu: This time, we sign a deal we can keep.

117
00:12:14,307 --> 00:12:15,907
Construction begins.

118
00:12:17,507 --> 00:12:19,725
Enilec Mok: Every guarantee: recorded and capped.

119
00:12:21,325 --> 00:12:22,797
Red team question.

120
00:12:23,097 --> 00:12:32,356
If the plant's debt service coverage ratio falls below the covenant in year five, who moves first: the lender, the utility, or the Treasury?

121
00:12:33,956 --> 00:12:36,729
Belpau: Bankable is the start. Sustainable is the goal.

122
00:12:37,029 --> 00:12:39,056
The end of episode one.

123
00:12:40,506 --> 00:12:43,962
Ten questions every negotiator must answer.

124
00:12:45,562 --> 00:12:56,442
One. What exactly are we committing to when we sign an MoU? Two. Is there a real project behind the promise? Three. Who will buy the electricity, and how much do they need?

125
00:12:58,042 --> 00:13:07,877
Four. Can the utility actually pay? Five. What exactly does the PPA require us to pay? Six. Can the grid absorb the contracted electricity?

126
00:13:09,477 --> 00:13:17,775
Seven. Who carries currency, demand, grid and political risk? Eight. What is the maximum and the expected fiscal exposure?

127
00:13:19,375 --> 00:13:27,247
Nine. What happens if the project fails, or the contract terminates? And ten. What must be true before the government signs?

128
00:13:28,697 --> 00:13:32,132
Bankable is the start. Sustainable is the goal.
